computer_architecture.syllabus:
C programming language; x86-64 assembly language; Machine representation of programs;
Control structures for processors, register transfer level description of hardware; Hardware
description languages and simulation in Verilog; Instruction set design, instruction formats,
addressing modes, ISAs; A sequential x86-64 design; Processor pipelining, pipeline hazard
detection, stalling and forwarding; Out-of-order execution and program performance optimization;
RISC and CISC instruction sets (x86-64 and MIPS); Memory hierarchy and memory caches;
Microcontrollers for embedded applications; Alternative architectures.

principles_of_communication_systems.syllabus:
INTRODUCTION
Communication Systems; Analog and Digital Messages; Signal-to-Noise Ratio, the Channel
Bandwidth, and the Rate of Communication; Modulation.
REVIEW OF SIGNALS and SYSTEMS
Size of a signal; Classification of Signals; Signal Comparison: Correlation; Signal
Representation by Orthogonal Signal Set; Trigonometric Fourier Series; A Periodic Signal
Representation by Fourier Integral; Signal Transmission through a Linear System; Signal
Distortion over a Communication Channel; Signal Energy and Energy Spectral Density;
Signal Power and Power Spectral Density; Noise Temperature; Thermal Noise and White
Noise;
BASEBAND SIGNAL TRANSMISSION
Systems Parameters and Signal Distortion in Baseband Transmission; Linear Distortion;
Equalization; Nonlinear Distortion and Companding; Signal-to-Noise Ratio in Different Parts
of Communication System; Signal-to-Noise Ratio in System with Repeaters.
ANALOG MODULATION METHODS
Baseband and Carrier Communication; Amplitude Modulation; AM, DSB, SSB, VSB;
Carrier Acquisition; Super-Heterodyne AM Receiver; Methods of Generation and
Demodulation in Linear Modulations; FM; PM; Bandwidth of Angle-Modulated Wave;
Methods of Generation and Demodulation in Non-linear Modulations; NBFM and WBFM;
Noise in Analog Communication Systems
Introduction; Noise in Baseband Systems; Signal-to-Noise ratio in PM and FM; Noise in
Linear and Non-linear modulation systems; Noise in DSB, SSB and AM; Preemphasis/
Deemphasis Filtering; Interference in Linear and Non-linear Modulation; FDM and TDM;

electrical_circuits_1.syllabus:
Coulomb's, Ohm's, and Kirchhoff’s laws; electrostatics and electromagnetics; resistance,
capacitance, inductance; series and parallel circuits, independent and dependent voltage and
current sources; energy, power; Superposition, Thevenin, and Norton Theorems; DC and AC;
initial, steady state and transient conditions; frequency selective circuits and resonance;
poly-phase circuits and transformers.

digital_communications.syllabus:
Introduction
Introduction to digital communication; Review of the statistical methods in digital transmission;
Bandwidth of digital data;
Information Theory
Basic Concepts; Entropy; Source and Channel Coding; Capacity of AWGN Channels; Lossless
Compression Techniques;
Pulse Modulation
Sampling; PAM, PPM, PDM; Pulse Code Modulation;
Baseband Data Transmission
ISI; Nyquist Criterion; Probability of Error Performance; Optimum Transmitting and Receiving
Filters; Pulse Shaping; Doubinary System; M-ary Systems; Spectra of Digital Baseband Signals;
Bandwidth of Digital Baseband Signals; Equalization; Scrambling; Eye Diagrams;
Synchronization Methods;
Digital Information Transmission Using Carrier Modulation
Introduction, ASK, PSK, FSK, DPSK; Matched Filter; Coherent and Non-coherent
Demodulation of Binary Digital Carrier Signals; Quadrature Modulation Schemes; Minimum
Shift Keying; M-ary Modulation (MFSK, MPSK, MQAM); Synchronization Methods;
Digital Transmission of Analog Signals
Sampling Theory and Practice; Quantization; Uniform and Non-uniform Quantization; Aliasing;
mu-law and A-law Quantization; Quantization of Noise; PCM and SNR of PCM; Delta
Modulation and SNR of DM; DPCM; LPC speech Synthesis; TDM and FDM; TDM/PCM;
Comparison PCM versus Analog Modulation;
Error Control Coding
Type of errors; Type of Codes and Error Corrections; Block Codes; Cyclic Codes; Convolutional
Codes; ARQ and FEC methods;
